Hi Maria,

I think the answer might depend a bit on what exactly you’re looking for.  If you want to interpret the parameter estimate associated with your 2-level predictor as the main effect in the traditional ANOVA sense (that is, the effect of that predictor averaged over all levels of all other predictors),  and your model includes the interaction of that predictor with any other predictors, then it is crucial that you use a “true” (sum-to-zero) contrast, such as contr.sum or contr.helmert, for all *other* predictors that your 2-level predictor is interacting with in your model specification.  It is actually not crucial what coding scheme you use for your 2-level predictor.

You originally specified your question in the following way:

  "Does sum contrast coding address any imbalance in my data?"

and I’m not answering that question directly because I’m not sure exactly what you mean by “address”.

You might also want to read this short paper I’ve written on how to work with and interpret main effects in the presence of higher-order interactions in R:

  http://arxiv.org/abs/1405.2094

especially the first two paragraphs of Section 1.

Best & I hope this helps!

Roger


